Always release file-based locks in a finally block to prevent lock leaks if the critical section throws.
Wrapping any resource acquisition (locks, connections, handles) that has an explicit release method.
In `src/commands/moor.ts`, the original code called `lock.release()` after `reserve()`. If `reserve` threw an error, the lock would never be released, causing a deadlock for subsequent attempts.

The fix is to wrap the critical section in a try/finally block:
```typescript
try {
  await reserve(name, opts);
} finally {
  lock.release();
}
```
This ensures the lock is always released regardless of success or failure.
